▶ Running Scenario: Desired State — Default Negotiation and Zero-Deployment Manifest (device-core-manifest-semantics)
  Description: Per docs.margo.org/specification/margo-management-interface/desired-state: (1) the WFM (here, our mock server) MUST default to application/vnd.margo.manifest.v1+json when the Accept header is omitted entirely, not just when it's the exact expected value; (2) when a client has zero deployments assigned, the manifest's deployments field MUST still be a valid (empty) array. Both are edge cases the other device-supplier groups don't exercise directly. Note: the spec also requires the manifest's bundle field to be explicit null (not omitted) when deployments is empty — not independently verified here, because this suite's validation engine can't distinguish a field that's present-but-null from one that's absent (both read as Go/JS nil); confirmed instead by reading the mock server's response-construction code directly.
  → Step: Get Deployments With No Accept Header — Defaults To Manifest Format
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: Reset Desired State To Empty (test-control)
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: Get Deployments — Zero Deployments Still Returns A Valid (Empty) Manifest
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: Cleanup: Restore Default Deployment
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)

▶ Running Scenario: MI-004 — Device Certificate Must Conform To RFC 5280 (device-mi004-rfc5280-certificate)
  Description: MARGO-DEV-MANAGEMENTINTERFACE-004: all client SVIDs MUST conform to RFC 5280. Under MIAF there is no onboarding call to reject a bad cert at -- a non-conformant certificate fails the mTLS handshake itself before any request is processed, and MIS's minter only issues conformant SVIDs, so there is currently no tooling to fabricate a non-conformant one to test the negative case. This step positively confirms our SVID (RFC-5280-conformant by construction) completes a real mTLS handshake and is accepted. NEGATIVE CASE RETIRED -- needs a way to mint/fabricate a non-conformant SVID; tracked as a follow-up, not silently dropped.
  → Step: Connect With A Conforming SVID Over mTLS — Accepted
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)

▶ Running Scenario: MI-006/009 — Bundle Digest That Doesn't Match Content Must Be Rejected (device-mi006-bundle-digest-mismatch)
  Description: SUITE SELF-TEST for MARGO-DEV-MANAGEMENTINTERFACE-006 / -009: the client MUST verify computed digests match manifest values and abort on any mismatch. The 'bundle_digest_mismatch' fixture corrupts bundle.digest while leaving bundle.url pointing at the real archive; evaluateManifest downloads it, hashes it, and must flag the mismatch.
  → Step: Arm bundle_digest_mismatch Fixture
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: GET Deployments — Bundle Digest Mismatch Must Be Rejected
    ✅ manifest correctly flagged as non-conformant: bundle: digest mismatch — manifest says sha256:cc106b4c5bb872e96b49d5217d5f645d92b38b54e755decbf529c1c5b0003c60, content is sha256:cc106b4c5bb872e96b49d5217d5f645d92b38b54e755decbf529c1c5b0003c6a (MI-006/019)
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: Cleanup: Restore Default Deployment And Clear Fixture
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)

▶ Running Scenario: MI-008 — Manifest With Unsupported Digest Algorithm Must Be Rejected (device-mi008-unsupported-digest-algorithm)
  Description: SUITE SELF-TEST for MARGO-DEV-MANAGEMENTINTERFACE-008 ('If a manifest references an unsupported digest algorithm, the client MUST treat the manifest as invalid and abort processing'). The mock is armed with the 'unsupported_digest_algorithm' negative fixture so GET /deployments returns a manifest whose digests use sha512 instead of sha256. The suite then asserts (a) the fixture is actually present in the response and (b) its own manifest-conformance check (expect_manifest_rejected) flags the manifest for rejection. This proves the suite would correctly fail a real device that acted on such a manifest.
  → Step: Arm The unsupported_digest_algorithm Negative Fixture
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: GET Deployments — Manifest Uses An Unsupported Digest Algorithm And Must Be Rejected
    ✅ manifest correctly flagged as non-conformant: bundle.digest uses an unsupported algorithm: "sha512:c82f87857ab82b1dd482fa42a87520090fbfce35738a9b8eb0aaa7f6746252b1" (MI-008)
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)
  → Step: Cleanup: Restore Default Deployment And Clear Fixture
    ✅ PASS - HTTP 200 (Expected: 200)

